  "answer": "### Short Answer\nIndividuals exempt from filing an income tax return under the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC) include minimum wage earners and those whose gross sales or receipts do not exceed the VAT threshold.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe relevant provisions are found in Section 24 of the NIRC, which states that minimum wage earners are exempt from income tax on their taxable income, including holiday pay, overtime pay, night shift differential pay, and hazard pay. Additionally, self-employed individuals or professionals whose gross sales or gross receipts do not exceed the VAT threshold are also exempt from filing.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 24 of the NIRC specifies that minimum wage earners, as defined in Section 22(HH), are not required to pay income tax on their earnings. This exemption extends to certain additional compensations they receive. Furthermore, self-employed individuals or professionals can opt for an 8% tax rate on gross sales or receipts if their income does not exceed the VAT threshold, thereby exempting them from the graduated income tax rates.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practical terms, this means that individuals earning below the minimum wage or those whose business income is minimal are relieved from the burden of filing income tax returns. This provision aims to simplify tax compliance for low-income earners and encourage small businesses by reducing their tax obligations.",
